# Byte-identical checkouts on every OS (BLD-19). The content pack hash
# (blindside-content) and the replay fixtures are hashes over file bytes, so a CRLF
# checkout on Windows would change every recorded hash. Anything binary that ever lands
# under content/ needs its own `binary` line here, above the content/** rule.
*.rs        text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
# Cargo.lock is authoritative (every CI step passes --locked) and has no extension
# the rules above match. It is LF in the index and in the working copy today; this
# line stops a Windows checkout converting it and is a no-op otherwise.
Cargo.lock  text eol=lf
content/**  text eol=lf
fixtures/** text eol=lf
# Shell scripts are run by bash on all three CI OSes; a CRLF checkout makes bash fail on
# the shebang line with `$'\r': command not found`, which is a confusing way to learn about
# line endings at 1am. Same reason for ci/**, which is read line by line by shell steps in
# .github/workflows: a CR at the end of a line would be passed through to
# `cargo tree -p <crate>` as part of the crate name.
*.sh        text eol=lf
ci/**       text eol=lf
# Dotfiles with no extension the rules above match. Not hash-relevant, but `core.autocrlf`
# is on for this repository, so without these two lines git rewrites them to CRLF on a
# Windows checkout and every Linux collaborator sees a whole-file phantom diff.
.gitignore     text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
